@node "Installation" "MEM.LDG : Installation"

	Just copy the file MEM.LDG into your LDG-folder. If
	you haven't installed the LDG-system, please read the 
	LDG documentation first. Copy the mem.h file in your
	include\ldg folder. Compile mem.c as a C-library and
	copy it in your lib folder using the project file or
	Makefile (depending on your compiler).

@endnode

@node "Purpose" "MEM.lDG : Purpose"

	The goal of this library is to prodive to other LDG-libraries 
	some high-level memory functions. LDG-libraries cannot use 
	directly the C malloc() function because LDG needs usually to 
	share the memory reserved by malloc() with its client. Use 
	directly the function ldg_Malloc() (ldg_Malloc() gets memory 
	directly from the system using the Malloc() and Mxalloc() 
	GEMDOS functions) is a bad idea if you need to allocate or 
	desallocate a lot of time because the GEMDOS memory function 
	are bugged and contribute to fragment the memory.

	So, MEM.LDG offers the equivalent functions to malloc(),
	realloc(), free()... The memory obtained with these functions
	can be shared with others programs.

@endnode

@node "Credits" "MEM.LDG : Credits"

	This LDG uses the functions (modified) of MiNTLIB.

@endnode

@node "Functions" "MEM.LDG : Functions"

	With version 0.50, each fonction admits an additionnal 
	parameter (a context) which identify client which use
	MEM.LDG. This parameter allows MEM.LDG to allocate 
	separate bloc memory for each process. 
	
	Functions are prefixed with an underscore character '_'. 
	Functions without context parameter have no prefix and 
	are binded in mem.h using the AES application identificator
	as context.

	Rem: if we use the prefixed function with an unique context
	     (0 for example) and if we set the memory allocation mode
	     to GLOBAL (see function ldg_allocmode()), the library
	     works exactly as in version 0.40 (see Historic).

	#include <ldg/mem.h>
	
	void* _ldg_malloc( long size, long context);
	void*  ldg_malloc( long size);
	
		Prodived shared memory in a similar way than the malloc() 
		function.

	void* _ldg_calloc( long size, long context);
	void*  ldg_calloc( long size);

		Similar to ldg_malloc() excepted the memory is initialized
		to zero.
	
	void* _ldg_realloc( void *adr, long size);
	void*  ldg_realloc( void *adr, long size);
	
		Dynamic reallocation of memory obtained with 
		ldg_malloc() (see realloc()).

	void _ldg_free( void *adr, long context);
	void  ldg_free( void *adr);
	
		Release the shared memory reserved with ldg_malloc().

	void _ldg_strdup( void *src, long context);
	void  ldg_strdup( void *src);

		Duplicate a string.

	void _ldg_allocmode( long mode, long context);
	void  ldg_allocmode( long mode);

		mode = 1 : set memory allocation to global mode
		mode = 0 : set memory allocation to private mode (default)
	
		This function should be called before any memory allocation
		requests. As MEM.LDG is a memory manager, it allocates a 
		large bloc of memory. So this bloc cannot be allocated in 
		private and global mode both. 
		In global mode, bloc memory are locked by the LDG deamon
		(see Historic version 0.40 for explanation).

@endnode

@node "Binding" "MEM.LDG : Binding"

    #include <ldg/mem.h>
    LDG *mem;
	int err, vers = 0x0050;

	err = ldg_mem_init( "mem.ldg", vers, &mem);

	/* err is a LDG code error (see ldg.hyp)
	 * vers is a number version required for mem.ldg 
	 */
	 
    /* compile and link your library with -lmem flag */
    
    /* At the end, you have to free up the library */
    ldg_close( mem, ldg_global);

@endnode

@node "Bugs" "MEM.LDG : Bugs"

	Currently, any bugs are reported.

@endnode

@node "Historic and futur" "MEM.LDG : Historic and futur"

	With LDG.MEM, we have a global memory manager for all 
	LDG-library. It could be interesting to create some controls
	of this function, for example :

		- free memory used by crashed library (this problem
		  is overcome with MEM version 0.50)

		- set a maximum limit per client or for all clients

		- choice between TTram and STram ...
	
	Any ideas are welcome.

Historic:

version 0.50 :
	MEM.LDG allocates now separate memory for each client.
	Each client has its own memory (this memory was prevously
	common to all clients), it has two consequences :
	  - memory depassement is less dangerous for others process 
	    clients,
	  - the recurrent problem of memory system desallocation
	  	when a process finished has no consequence.

	By default
		
version 0.40 : (not public release)
	The 14-bit mode of Mxalloc() was removed because memory
	reserved can never be desallocated. To prevent this problem,	
	shareable memories are locked by the LDG deamon. The LDG
	deamon version 1.10 is absolutely required (provided by
	LDG 2.30 package).

version 0.31 :
	Adding ldg_strdup() function.

version 0.30 :
	The library is now shareable because we use the 14-bit 
	mode of Mxalloc() : this bit prevent the system to 
	desalloc memory reserved by applications having
	terminated.
	
version 0.20 :
	The library is unshareable to prevent the memory desallocation 
	performed by the OS when the first client terms (this problem 
	occurs with MiNT).

version 0.10 :
	First public released.

@endnode

